        "math_academy_id": "q-279485",
        "problem": "Consider the matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ If $f(X)={X}^{2}-X-2$, find $f(D).$\n\n| $f(D)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |",
        "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-X-2 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-X-2I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(D)={D}^{2}-D-2I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${D}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(D):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & ={D}^{2}-D-2I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9-3-2 & -8-(-2)-0 \\\\ 0-0-0 & 1-1-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
